WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.047
Rust separates text from bytes in a way
that supports defensive programming.

2
00:00:04.127 --> 00:00:05.656
A `String` owns valid UTF-8 text.

3
00:00:05.736 --> 00:00:07.373
An `&str` borrows valid UTF-8 text.

4
00:00:07.453 --> 00:00:09.309
A byte slice may contain arbitrary data.

5
00:00:09.389 --> 00:00:13.318
Treating arbitrary bytes as text before
validation creates confusion about length,

6
00:00:13.398 --> 00:00:15.745
encoding, delimiters, and character
boundaries.

7
00:00:15.825 --> 00:00:19.700
This distinction matters because text
length and byte length are not always the same

8
00:00:19.780 --> 00:00:20.216
concept.

9
00:00:20.296 --> 00:00:23.462
Code that slices text as if every
character were one byte can be wrong.

10
00:00:23.542 --> 00:00:24.797
Safe Rust protects memory,

11
00:00:24.877 --> 00:00:28.698
but the application still needs correct
parsing and validation so text operations

12
00:00:28.778 --> 00:00:31.998
match the protocol, file format, or
business rule being implemented.

13
00:00:32.078 --> 00:00:35.461
File input, network input, protocol
messages, command-line arguments,

14
00:00:35.541 --> 00:00:38.543
and serialized data should be treated as
untrusted until parsed.

15
00:00:38.623 --> 00:00:42.061
A parser boundary should validate length,
format, encoding, delimiters,

16
00:00:42.141 --> 00:00:45.525
record structure, and supported values
before data is used for indexing,

17
00:00:45.605 --> 00:00:48.934
allocation, file paths, database
operations, or downstream requests.

18
00:00:49.014 --> 00:00:51.088
Parser boundaries are security boundaries.

19
00:00:51.168 --> 00:00:54.497
They are the place where raw input becomes
structured application data.

20
00:00:54.577 --> 00:00:57.143
Defensive code should keep raw bytes near
the boundary,

21
00:00:57.223 --> 00:00:59.187
convert them into validated domain types,

22
00:00:59.267 --> 00:01:03.197
and avoid letting untrusted strings or
slices drift through the application without

23
00:01:03.277 --> 00:01:03.713
context.

24
00:01:03.793 --> 00:01:05.922
Good parser design also defines safe
failure.

25
00:01:06.002 --> 00:01:09.386
Invalid encoding, truncated data, extra
delimiters, oversized fields,

26
00:01:09.466 --> 00:01:13.395
missing terminators, and unsupported
versions should produce predictable errors.

27
00:01:13.475 --> 00:01:16.968
That makes the application more reliable
and reduces pressure to add unsafe

28
00:01:17.048 --> 00:01:18.740
shortcuts around difficult inputs.
